Before class, I read through my client’s project brief.
Liz Miller provided my team with a lot of resources about her research, including website links, images,
and examples of other artists’ work for inspiration. 
Even though we were given a lot of information, I was still somewhat confused about the main goal of the project. 
One of the questions I had was: How can we emphasize the inter-relationships between the islands and the islanders?

During class, Elio gave us a lecture about ideation, which helped us to brainstorm for our projects. 
We grabbed a big piece of paper and spent about 20 minutes writing down the most important things for our project 
such as the client’s goal, research about the islands, and resources provided.

Once that was done, we discussed about it and took a new sheet of paper for the next step. 
This time, we jotted down our questions, our concerns, cool features we wanted to include, 
and visual ideas, like colors, styles, and layouts to help create what our final project could look like.

For this project, I was mostly concerned if my team would be responsible for researching the Hochelaga Archipelago in detail;
to include information for every 250 islands in the project. 
And since this project is for a documentary, I am assuming that we would have to incorporate a large amount of text, 
images and videos in it, hoping the information (design) will not look too busy or scattered everywhere. 
I think that organizing the content all together while making it visually engaging will be an important challenge for our team.

